1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,162
How was penicillin discovered? By accident.

2
00:00:04,612 --> 00:00:07,817
In September 1928, a London bacteriologist named

3
00:00:07,817 --> 00:00:11,157
Alexander Fleming came back from a summer vacation

4
00:00:11,157 --> 00:00:14,429
and found that a mold had contaminated one of his

5
00:00:14,429 --> 00:00:15,698
dishes of bacteria.

6
00:00:15,698 --> 00:00:18,570
Around the mold, the bacteria did not grow.

7
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:22,579
16 years later, American companies had produced 2.3

8
00:00:22,579 --> 00:00:26,139
million doses of the drug first found in that mold.

9
00:00:26,139 --> 00:00:27,395
In time for D-Day.

10
00:00:27,845 --> 00:00:30,653
Between the dish and the beaches were a team in

11
00:00:30,653 --> 00:00:33,640
Oxford, a moldy cantaloupe in Illinois, and an old

12
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:34,954
ice plant in Brooklyn.

13
00:00:35,404 --> 00:00:38,271
This is the story of the first antibiotic to be

14
00:00:38,271 --> 00:00:39,125
mass-produced.

15
00:00:39,125 --> 00:00:42,114
And of the warning its discoverer gave the world.

16
00:00:42,514 --> 00:00:43,333
Penicillin.

17
00:00:43,333 --> 00:00:45,271
The mold that went to war.

18
00:00:47,671 --> 00:00:50,527
To see why it mattered, start with the First World War.

19
00:00:51,527 --> 00:00:54,486
Fleming served as a captain in the British Royal

20
00:00:54,486 --> 00:00:55,658
Army Medical Corps.

21
00:00:55,658 --> 00:00:58,803
He worked in a field laboratory in France, studying

22
00:00:58,803 --> 00:00:59,789
infected wounds.

23
00:01:00,239 --> 00:01:02,986
What he and his mentor, Almroth Wright, found was

24
00:01:02,986 --> 00:01:03,547
troubling.

25
00:01:03,547 --> 00:01:06,462
The antiseptics surgeons used in the field were more

26
00:01:06,462 --> 00:01:07,640
harmful than helpful.

27
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:10,331
They were often better at killing the body's own

28
00:01:10,331 --> 00:01:13,414
infection-fighting white blood cells than the bacteria.

29
00:01:13,864 --> 00:01:16,847
Doctors had no drug that could clear a wound

30
00:01:16,847 --> 00:01:18,949
infection from inside the body.

31
00:01:18,949 --> 00:01:22,272
And in the Great War, disease caused about 1 in 3

32
00:01:22,272 --> 00:01:23,357
military deaths.

33
00:01:23,957 --> 00:01:27,474
After the war, Fleming went back to his laboratory at St.

34
00:01:27,474 --> 00:01:29,078
Mary's Hospital in London.

35
00:01:29,078 --> 00:01:32,225
10 years later, a mistake would eventually make him

36
00:01:32,225 --> 00:01:32,657
famous.

37
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:34,627
It began with a dirty bench.

38
00:01:35,627 --> 00:01:38,892
In the summer of 1928, Fleming went on vacation to

39
00:01:38,892 --> 00:01:39,480
Scotland.

40
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:43,137
He left some of his bacterial cultures out on the bench.

41
00:01:43,587 --> 00:01:46,600
A culture is a dish of jelly where bacteria are

42
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:47,689
grown on purpose.

43
00:01:47,689 --> 00:01:50,894
This is what they look like in a lab today, at the

44
00:01:50,894 --> 00:01:53,394
Walter Reed Army Institute of Research.

45
00:01:53,844 --> 00:01:57,070
Fleming's dishes held staphylococcus, a common bacterium.

46
00:01:57,070 --> 00:01:58,994
On one of them, a mold had landed.

47
00:01:58,994 --> 00:02:01,768
And around the mold was a ring where the bacteria

48
00:02:01,768 --> 00:02:02,503
did not grow.

49
00:02:02,953 --> 00:02:04,505
The mold was a Penicillium.

50
00:02:04,505 --> 00:02:07,437
The bacteria-killing substance it made became known

51
00:02:07,437 --> 00:02:08,242
as penicillin.

52
00:02:08,692 --> 00:02:11,851
In 1929, Fleming published his findings in the

53
00:02:11,851 --> 00:02:14,735
British Journal of Experimental Pathology.

54
00:02:14,735 --> 00:02:16,795
The paper was largely ignored.

55
00:02:17,245 --> 00:02:19,752
Fleming did little more work on it.

56
00:02:19,752 --> 00:02:22,831
But he kept his mold growing. For 12 years.

57
00:02:23,281 --> 00:02:26,242
A line often attributed to him puts it this way:

58
00:02:26,442 --> 00:02:29,109
I certainly didn't plan to revolutionize all

59
00:02:29,109 --> 00:02:32,321
medicine by discovering the world's first antibiotic.

60
00:02:33,121 --> 00:02:34,101
The mold waited.

61
00:02:34,101 --> 00:02:36,551
Then, in Oxford, someone read the paper.

62
00:02:37,551 --> 00:02:40,814
In 1938, the pathologist Howard Florey and the

63
00:02:40,814 --> 00:02:44,360
biochemist Ernst Chain decided to study substances

64
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:45,708
that kill bacteria.

65
00:02:45,708 --> 00:02:47,623
Penicillin was one of them.

66
00:02:48,073 --> 00:02:51,198
A third biochemist, Norman Heatley, found ways to

67
00:02:51,198 --> 00:02:52,410
grow and purify it.

68
00:02:52,410 --> 00:02:55,599
The lab grew the mold practically everywhere, from

69
00:02:55,599 --> 00:02:56,874
bedpans to bathtubs.

70
00:02:56,874 --> 00:02:59,872
Six women technicians, nicknamed the penicillin

71
00:02:59,872 --> 00:03:00,957
girls, tended it.

72
00:03:01,407 --> 00:03:05,548
On May 25, 1940, Florey's team tested penicillin on

73
00:03:05,548 --> 00:03:06,684
infected mice.

74
00:03:06,684 --> 00:03:09,445
The mice it was given to survived.

75
00:03:09,895 --> 00:03:12,322
That August, Florey and Chain reported their

76
00:03:12,322 --> 00:03:13,590
findings in the Lancet.

77
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:16,348
Research groups around the world were electrified.

78
00:03:16,798 --> 00:03:18,490
Next came a human patient.

79
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:21,485
In February 1941, a policeman became the first

80
00:03:21,485 --> 00:03:24,610
patient with an infection to be treated with the

81
00:03:24,610 --> 00:03:26,823
Oxford team's purified penicillin.

82
00:03:27,273 --> 00:03:28,834
At first, he improved.

83
00:03:28,834 --> 00:03:32,310
There was so little penicillin that the team even

84
00:03:32,310 --> 00:03:35,218
recovered it from his urine to use again.

85
00:03:35,218 --> 00:03:36,424
It wasn't enough.

86
00:03:36,424 --> 00:03:37,985
He relapsed, and died.

87
00:03:38,435 --> 00:03:41,752
Making enough penicillin for one human case took

88
00:03:41,752 --> 00:03:44,584
about 2,000 liters of mold culture fluid.

89
00:03:44,984 --> 00:03:47,487
Early purification was just as hard.

90
00:03:47,487 --> 00:03:50,546
Many gallons of mold broth gave an amount of

91
00:03:50,546 --> 00:03:54,092
penicillin just large enough to cover a fingernail.

92
00:03:54,492 --> 00:03:55,888
Britain was at war.

93
00:03:55,888 --> 00:03:59,709
So at the beginning of July 1941, Florey and Heatley

94
00:03:59,709 --> 00:04:03,383
flew across the Atlantic, to ask America for help.

95
00:04:03,983 --> 00:04:04,950
America answered.

96
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:06,712
In the middle of the corn belt.

97
00:04:07,712 --> 00:04:11,133
They were sent to the Northern Regional Research

98
00:04:11,133 --> 00:04:12,700
Laboratory of the U.S.

99
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:16,050
Department of Agriculture, in Peoria, Illinois.

100
00:04:16,050 --> 00:04:18,187
They arrived on July 14, 1941.

101
00:04:18,187 --> 00:04:20,254
Work began the very next day.

102
00:04:20,704 --> 00:04:24,128
Until then, the mold had been grown on the surface

103
00:04:24,128 --> 00:04:27,485
of liquid, in flasks and bottles, like these in a

104
00:04:27,485 --> 00:04:28,443
1944 newsreel.

105
00:04:28,893 --> 00:04:31,872
Peoria's scientists grew it submerged instead, in

106
00:04:31,872 --> 00:04:34,972
deep vats of corn steep liquor, a byproduct of corn

107
00:04:34,972 --> 00:04:36,674
milling, with air pumped in.

108
00:04:36,674 --> 00:04:39,167
It grew faster, and made more penicillin.

109
00:04:39,617 --> 00:04:41,507
They also needed a better mold.

110
00:04:41,507 --> 00:04:44,557
Lab workers collected samples from produce markets

111
00:04:44,557 --> 00:04:46,020
and other local sources.

112
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:49,624
The winner came from a moldy cantaloupe found at a

113
00:04:49,624 --> 00:04:50,507
Peoria market.

114
00:04:50,507 --> 00:04:53,787
Its strain became the parent of all the strains used

115
00:04:53,787 --> 00:04:54,606
commercially.

116
00:04:55,056 --> 00:04:55,983
Who found it?

117
00:04:55,983 --> 00:04:59,619
A lab technician named Mary Hunt is often credited,

118
00:04:59,619 --> 00:05:02,042
and the nickname Moldy Mary stuck.

119
00:05:02,042 --> 00:05:05,535
But the USDA itself says the finder is uncertain.

120
00:05:05,985 --> 00:05:08,827
Scientists then pushed the mold to change.

121
00:05:08,827 --> 00:05:12,141
At the University of Wisconsin, ultraviolet light

122
00:05:12,141 --> 00:05:14,644
helped produce a strain called Q-176.

123
00:05:14,644 --> 00:05:18,094
It became the most productive strain of the wartime

124
00:05:18,094 --> 00:05:18,635
program.

125
00:05:19,085 --> 00:05:21,233
Norman Heatley stayed on in America.

126
00:05:21,233 --> 00:05:24,157
He later worked at the drug company Merck, on the

127
00:05:24,157 --> 00:05:26,484
problems of making penicillin at scale.

128
00:05:26,934 --> 00:05:28,994
Meanwhile, drug companies joined in.

129
00:05:28,994 --> 00:05:30,310
At first, four of them:

130
00:05:30,310 --> 00:05:32,256
Merck, Squibb, Pfizer and Lederle.

131
00:05:32,256 --> 00:05:34,832
After the attack on Pearl Harbor, 21 American

132
00:05:34,832 --> 00:05:36,721
companies combined their efforts.

133
00:05:37,171 --> 00:05:40,410
In March 1942, a woman near death in a hospital in

134
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:43,714
New Haven, Connecticut, became the first patient in

135
00:05:43,714 --> 00:05:47,277
the United States successfully treated with penicillin.

136
00:05:47,727 --> 00:05:51,956
In Brooklyn, the company Pfizer converted an old ice

137
00:05:51,956 --> 00:05:55,941
plant into a penicillin factory, with 14 tanks of

138
00:05:55,941 --> 00:05:57,486
7,500 gallons each.

139
00:05:57,486 --> 00:05:59,681
It opened on March 1, 1944.

140
00:06:00,131 --> 00:06:03,562
Soon, it was making more penicillin in one month

141
00:06:03,562 --> 00:06:06,491
than the company had made in all of 1943.

142
00:06:07,091 --> 00:06:10,840
By the spring of 1944, the Allies were getting ready

143
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:12,209
to invade Normandy.

144
00:06:13,209 --> 00:06:16,441
In English ports, American troops boarded landing ships.

145
00:06:16,891 --> 00:06:20,806
Ahead of D-Day, 21 American companies had produced

146
00:06:20,806 --> 00:06:23,312
2.3 million doses of penicillin.

147
00:06:23,762 --> 00:06:26,802
According to the American Chemical Society, most of

148
00:06:26,802 --> 00:06:29,782
the penicillin that went ashore with Allied forces

149
00:06:29,782 --> 00:06:33,119
on D-Day came from that converted ice plant in Brooklyn.

150
00:06:33,569 --> 00:06:37,861
On June 6, 1944, Allied troops landed in Normandy.

151
00:06:38,311 --> 00:06:42,043
Behind them came the field hospitals. This U.S.

152
00:06:42,043 --> 00:06:46,014
Army hospital was set up in Normandy in July 1944.

153
00:06:46,464 --> 00:06:48,955
The drug was getting cheaper fast.

154
00:06:48,955 --> 00:06:52,179
In July 1943, a dose of penicillin cost $20.

155
00:06:52,179 --> 00:06:54,817
Three years later, it cost 55 cents.

156
00:06:55,267 --> 00:06:59,220
From January to May of 1942, 400 million units of

157
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:01,317
pure penicillin were made.

158
00:07:01,317 --> 00:07:05,028
By the end of the war, American companies were

159
00:07:05,028 --> 00:07:07,932
producing 650 billion units a month.

160
00:07:08,382 --> 00:07:09,661
One more number.

161
00:07:09,661 --> 00:07:13,419
In World War One, the death rate from bacterial

162
00:07:13,419 --> 00:07:14,858
pneumonia was 18%.

163
00:07:14,858 --> 00:07:18,296
In World War Two, with sulfa drugs and then

164
00:07:18,296 --> 00:07:20,854
penicillin, it was less than 1%.

165
00:07:21,304 --> 00:07:23,884
The early publicity went mostly to Fleming.

166
00:07:23,884 --> 00:07:26,824
The Oxford group's part was largely ignored, even

167
00:07:26,824 --> 00:07:29,944
though they were the ones who turned penicillin into

168
00:07:29,944 --> 00:07:30,604
a medicine.

169
00:07:31,054 --> 00:07:34,927
In 1945, Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel

170
00:07:34,927 --> 00:07:37,358
Prize in Physiology or Medicine.

171
00:07:37,358 --> 00:07:39,485
Norman Heatley was left out.

172
00:07:39,485 --> 00:07:43,206
In 1990, Oxford gave him an honorary doctorate of

173
00:07:43,206 --> 00:07:43,890
medicine.

174
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:46,496
But the story doesn't end with a prize.

175
00:07:47,496 --> 00:07:52,069
On December 11, 1945, Fleming gave his Nobel lecture.

176
00:07:52,069 --> 00:07:54,399
He ended it with a warning.

177
00:07:54,849 --> 00:07:57,637
The time may come, he said, when penicillin can be

178
00:07:57,637 --> 00:07:59,310
bought by anyone in the shops.

179
00:07:59,310 --> 00:08:01,930
He feared that people who took too little of it

180
00:08:01,930 --> 00:08:03,938
would breed microbes that resist it.

181
00:08:04,388 --> 00:08:06,843
He even described an imagined case.

182
00:08:06,843 --> 00:08:09,229
A man takes too little penicillin.

183
00:08:09,229 --> 00:08:11,333
His microbes become resistant.

184
00:08:11,333 --> 00:08:13,228
He passes them to his wife.

185
00:08:13,228 --> 00:08:17,226
She develops pneumonia, and penicillin fails to save her.

186
00:08:17,676 --> 00:08:19,289
He had reason to worry.

187
00:08:19,289 --> 00:08:22,864
In 1940, Edward Abraham and Ernst Chain had already

188
00:08:22,864 --> 00:08:26,299
described an enzyme from bacteria able to destroy

189
00:08:26,299 --> 00:08:27,070
penicillin.

190
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:29,203
So how does penicillin work?

191
00:08:29,203 --> 00:08:31,668
Many bacteria are wrapped in a cell wall.

192
00:08:31,668 --> 00:08:34,434
Penicillin binds to the enzymes that build it,

193
00:08:34,434 --> 00:08:36,538
called penicillin-binding proteins.

194
00:08:36,538 --> 00:08:37,560
The wall weakens.

195
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:38,522
Water pushes in.

196
00:08:38,522 --> 00:08:39,724
And the cell bursts.

197
00:08:40,174 --> 00:08:41,722
Some bacteria fight back.

198
00:08:41,722 --> 00:08:44,694
They make an enzyme, beta-lactamase, that breaks

199
00:08:44,694 --> 00:08:47,233
down part of the drug before it can work.

200
00:08:47,683 --> 00:08:51,667
The CDC estimates that more than 2.8 million

201
00:08:51,667 --> 00:08:56,374
antimicrobial-resistant infections occur in the U.S.

202
00:08:56,374 --> 00:09:01,082
each year, and that more than 35,000 people die as a

203
00:09:01,082 --> 00:09:01,716
result.

204
00:09:02,166 --> 00:09:05,763
And in 2025, the World Health Organization reported

205
00:09:05,763 --> 00:09:08,725
that 1 in 6 laboratory-confirmed bacterial

206
00:09:08,725 --> 00:09:12,816
infections worldwide is resistant to standard antibiotics.

207
00:09:13,266 --> 00:09:15,733
The military tracks the problem too.

208
00:09:15,733 --> 00:09:19,159
At the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research, one

209
00:09:19,159 --> 00:09:22,516
network watches for antibiotic-resistant bacteria

210
00:09:22,516 --> 00:09:24,982
across the Army, Navy and Air Force.

211
00:09:25,432 --> 00:09:28,861
And military hospitals run their own microbiology

212
00:09:28,861 --> 00:09:32,639
labs, like this one at Naval Medical Center San Diego.

213
00:09:33,089 --> 00:09:37,772
World AMR Awareness Week runs from November 18 to 24.

214
00:09:37,772 --> 00:09:42,367
The World Health Organization's message this year is

215
00:09:42,367 --> 00:09:42,898
blunt.

216
00:09:42,898 --> 00:09:46,079
AMR is no longer a future challenge.

217
00:09:46,079 --> 00:09:48,023
It is a global threat.

218
00:09:48,623 --> 00:09:52,784
In 1928, a mold landed on a dish in London, and the

219
00:09:52,784 --> 00:09:55,639
paper about it was largely ignored.

220
00:09:56,089 --> 00:09:59,463
It took a team in Oxford, a lab in Peoria, a

221
00:09:59,463 --> 00:10:03,758
cantaloupe, and 21 companies to turn it into a medicine.

222
00:10:03,758 --> 00:10:05,138
In time for D-Day.

223
00:10:05,588 --> 00:10:08,936
Today, penicillin and the antibiotics that followed

224
00:10:08,936 --> 00:10:12,219
are so ordinary that it's easy to forget the world

225
00:10:12,219 --> 00:10:13,006
before them.

226
00:10:13,456 --> 00:10:16,280
And the man who noticed it first used his Nobel

227
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:18,863
lecture to warn that it could stop working.

228
00:10:19,463 --> 00:10:21,750
If this story surprised you, subscribe.

229
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:24,564
There are more stories on this channel about how

230
00:10:24,564 --> 00:10:26,206
medicine keeps people alive.

231
00:10:27,006 --> 00:10:28,146
For education only.

232
00:10:28,146 --> 00:10:29,767
This is not medical advice.

233
00:10:29,767 --> 00:10:32,588
If you have a health concern, talk to a doctor.
